# Universal Player Display Standards

Every player-facing game interface — current and future, including `apps/player` — is mobile-first and meets professional responsive application standards. The public TV display (`apps/display`) is a separate surface and stays under its own rules.

Every player gets the same gameplay information, controls, and functionality intended for them, on phones, tablets, foldables, and desktops, in portrait and landscape. Screen size, device type, and orientation do not remove access.

## Layout

- Adapt the layout. Do not hide, truncate, overlap, or discard important content.
- Fit active gameplay in the viewport. Vertical scrolling is allowed when it is genuinely necessary. Horizontal page scrolling is never required.
- When space is insufficient, redesign the layout. Do not remove functionality.
- Respect safe areas, browser toolbars, notches, and dynamic viewport size (`dvh` / `svh`, `env(safe-area-inset-*)`).

## Interaction and readability

- Text stays readable. Contrast stays accessible. Touch targets are at least 44×44 CSS pixels.
- Essential gameplay must not depend on hover, a device-specific feature, or one orientation.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — drops the bet control on small screens and forces sideways scroll
<div className="flex w-[480px]">
  <Hand />
  <button className="hidden max-sm:hidden">Bet</button>
</div>

// ✅ GOOD — same controls, stacked on a phone, no horizontal page scroll
<div className="flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col gap-3 sm:flex-row">
  <Hand />
  <button className="min-h-11 min-w-11">Bet</button>
</div>
```

## Mandatory three-stage responsive validation

Player-facing UI work is not complete until all three stages pass. After every change, revalidate every device that already passed, including devices this change did not target. A device is **PASS** only when that device and orientation were actually tested for this change. Otherwise report **NOT TESTED**.

Check widths **320, 360, 375, 390, and 430** pixels, plus tablet and desktop sizes, in both orientations. The surgical player-layout rule still sets its own required viewports, exceptions, and “do not add Playwright” limit. This rule does not replace those.

### 1. DOM and layout

Verify required elements exist, have valid dimensions (non-zero where the layout requires a box), and occupy the intended layout regions. Check overflow, clipping, inaccessible controls, and lost information.

DOM presence is not proof of visual visibility.

### 2. Rendered visibility and interaction

Use browser automation. Confirm each required control is actually visible, unobscured, inside the viewport, and interactable. A valid bounding box alone is not sufficient. Account for ancestor clipping (`overflow` hidden on a parent), overlays, and scrolling (including content reachable only after scroll).

### 3. Responsively visual validation

Capture and inspect actual screenshots across every configured device and both orientations, using the Responsively MCP integration (`list_devices`, `set_active_devices`, `navigate`, then `screenshot` for each active device). Inspect the images. A saved screenshot file is not a pass until it has been looked at.

Never automatically update screenshot baselines to suppress failures. Updating screenshots stays an explicit human step.

### Results

Report **PASS**, **FAIL**, or **NOT TESTED** for each device and orientation.

- If any mandatory element fails on any device, the overall result is **FAIL**.
- If the browser or Responsively tools are unavailable, report **BLOCKED**. Do not claim success.
- Do not commit, deploy, or claim completion while required tests fail.
